WB.L("trolley-problem", "philosophy", "beginner", "The trolley problem: would you pull the lever?", "A runaway tram, five people on the track and one lever in your hand. This simple puzzle has become one of the most argued-about scenarios in moral philosophy, and it now shapes how we think about self-driving cars.",
  [
    ["The puzzle", "The trolley problem asks whether it is right to divert a runaway tram so it kills one person instead of five.", "Picture yourself beside a railway line. A tram with failed brakes is hurtling towards five workers who cannot get out of the way. You are standing next to a lever. Pull it, and the tram swings onto a side track, where it will kill one worker instead. Do nothing, and five people die. Most people, when asked, say they would pull the lever. Five lives seem to outweigh one. But the puzzle is not really about trams. It is a carefully built thought experiment, a stripped-down scenario that isolates one question so we can look at our moral instincts under a bright light. The question here is whether the numbers are all that matter, or whether how a death comes about matters too."],
    ["Where it came from", "The British philosopher Philippa Foot introduced the scenario in 1967, and Judith Jarvis Thomson named and extended it.", "The puzzle began in a 1967 paper by the British philosopher Philippa Foot, who was writing about a much older idea called the doctrine of double effect. Her original version involved a driver steering a runaway tram, and she used it alongside other cases to probe the difference between killing someone and letting them die. In the 1970s and 1980s the American philosopher Judith Jarvis Thomson took up the example, gave it the name 'the trolley problem' and invented many of its famous variations. Since then it has escaped the seminar room. It appears in psychology experiments, television comedies, internet memes and engineering ethics courses. Few thought experiments have travelled so far, partly because anyone can grasp it in thirty seconds, yet it remains genuinely hard to answer."],
    ["The footbridge twist", "When saving five means pushing one person to their death, most people's answer flips.", "Thomson's most famous variation moves you onto a footbridge above the track. There is no lever this time. The only way to stop the tram is to push a very large stranger off the bridge into its path. His body would halt it, saving the five, but he would die. The arithmetic is identical: one life for five. Yet in surveys, most people who would pull the lever say they would not push the man. Many find the idea of pushing him deeply wrong, even if they struggle to explain why. That gap is what makes the puzzle so productive. If the numbers are the same, something else must be driving our judgement, and philosophers have spent decades trying to work out what it is, and whether it should count."],
    ["The case for counting lives", "Consequentialists judge actions by their outcomes, so they tend to see saving five as the right choice in both versions.", "One family of answers comes from consequentialism, the view that the rightness of an action depends on its results. The best known version, utilitarianism, associated with Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill, says we should aim for the greatest overall wellbeing. From this angle, the lever case is straightforward: five deaths are worse than one, so you should pull. A consistent consequentialist may say the same about the footbridge, arguing that our reluctance to push is a gut reaction, not a moral insight. Defenders point out that this approach is impartial and takes every life equally seriously. Critics reply that if outcomes are all that matter, it could justify terrible things, such as harvesting one healthy patient's organs to save five others, which almost everyone rejects."],
    ["The case for limits", "Other thinkers argue some things must not be done to a person, even to produce a better outcome.", "A rival tradition, often linked to the German philosopher Immanuel Kant, holds that people must never be treated merely as a means to an end. Pushing the man off the bridge uses his body as a tool to stop the tram, which looks like exactly that kind of treatment. Pulling the lever is different: the one worker dies as a side effect of redirecting the threat, not as the means of stopping it. This is the heart of the doctrine of double effect, which traces back to the medieval thinker Thomas Aquinas. It says that causing harm as a foreseen side effect can sometimes be permissible when causing the same harm intentionally would not be. Critics question whether that distinction can really carry so much moral weight."],
    ["What our brains are doing", "Psychologists have used the trolley problem to study how emotion and reasoning interact in moral choices.", "Around the start of this century, the psychologist and philosopher Joshua Greene and colleagues began scanning people's brains while they considered trolley dilemmas. Their work suggested that up close, personal harms like pushing someone tend to trigger stronger emotional responses, while impersonal cases like the lever invite more calculating reasoning. Greene proposed a 'dual process' account in which both systems compete. The findings have been debated, and some later studies have questioned how well the original results hold up. Philosophers also argue about what the science shows. Even if our reluctance to push comes from emotion, that does not settle whether the emotion is tracking something real, such as respect for persons, or merely reflecting an evolved discomfort with violence."],
    ["Trams become cars", "Self-driving cars have turned an old thought experiment into a practical design question.", "For decades critics complained that trolley problems were too artificial to matter. Then came autonomous vehicles. If a car's software must choose between swerving into one pedestrian or hitting several, someone has to decide in advance what it should do. A large online project from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, called the Moral Machine, collected millions of responses from people around the world to such scenarios and was published in 2018. It found broad agreement on sparing more lives and sparing humans over animals, but notable differences between regions on other questions. Many engineers stress that real crashes rarely look like neat dilemmas and that avoiding collisions altogether is the real goal. Still, the old puzzle now prompts serious discussion about who should set the rules for machines."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Who first introduced the scenario now known as the trolley problem?", ["Immanuel Kant", "John Stuart Mill", "Philippa Foot", "Joshua Greene"], 2, "Philippa Foot described it in a 1967 paper, and Judith Jarvis Thomson later named and extended it."],
    [2, "In the footbridge version, how do most people respond compared with the lever version?", ["They are more willing to act", "They are less willing to act", "They respond exactly the same", "They refuse to answer"], 1, "Although the numbers are identical, most people who would pull the lever say they would not push the man."],
    [3, "What does a consequentialist mainly use to judge whether an action is right?", ["Its outcomes", "The intentions of the actor", "Religious rules", "Tradition"], 0, "Consequentialism judges actions by their results, such as how much overall wellbeing they produce."],
    [4, "What does the doctrine of double effect say?", ["Every action has two equal effects", "Harm caused as a foreseen side effect can sometimes be allowed when intended harm would not", "Two wrongs can make a right", "Only the second effect of an action counts"], 1, "The doctrine distinguishes harm used as a means from harm that is a side effect of pursuing a good aim."],
    [6, "What was the Moral Machine?", ["A tram safety device", "A Kantian ethics textbook", "A brain scanner", "An online project collecting people's choices in self-driving car dilemmas"], 3, "The MIT project gathered millions of responses to car crash scenarios from people around the world."]
  ]);

WB.L("ship-of-theseus", "philosophy", "beginner", "The ship of Theseus: what makes you the same person?", "Replace every plank of a ship, one by one, and is it still the same ship? This ancient puzzle leads straight to one of the most personal questions in philosophy: what makes you, you, over a lifetime of change?",
  [
    ["A ship of new planks", "The ancient puzzle asks whether an object stays the same after all its parts are gradually replaced.", "The Greek writer Plutarch, around the turn of the second century, told of the ship in which the hero Theseus was said to have sailed home from Crete. The Athenians kept it for generations, he wrote, removing old timbers as they decayed and fitting new ones in their place. Philosophers, Plutarch said, used the ship as an example in arguments about growth: some said it remained the same ship, others that it did not. Imagine the process carried to the end, with not one original plank left. Is it still Theseus's ship? If you say yes, what makes it so? If you say no, at which plank did it stop being the original? Neither answer is as comfortable as it first seems."],
    ["Hobbes's twist", "Thomas Hobbes asked what happens if someone rebuilds the ship from all the discarded planks.", "In the 1600s the English philosopher Thomas Hobbes added a mischievous complication. Suppose someone collected every old plank as it was removed and later reassembled them, in the same order, into a ship. Now there are two ships. One sits in the harbour, continuously maintained, with no original material. The other is built entirely of the original timbers. Which one is the ship of Theseus? Both have a strong claim. One has continuity of history and use, the other has continuity of material. You might say both, or neither, or that the question has no single right answer. Hobbes's version shows that our everyday idea of 'the same thing' bundles together several different features that usually travel together but can come apart."],
    ["You are a ship too", "Your body constantly replaces material, which turns the puzzle into a question about personal identity.", "The puzzle becomes personal when you notice that you are a bit like that ship. Many of your body's cells are replaced over time, from skin to blood to the lining of your gut, although some, including most neurons in the brain, can last a lifetime. Your beliefs, tastes and memories change even more. The child in your earliest photos shares little with you in material, knowledge or personality. Yet you probably feel certain that child was you. Philosophers call this the problem of personal identity: what makes a person at one time the same person as someone at another time? The answer matters for everyday things, like holding someone responsible for a promise or a crime, and for deeper ones, like what it would mean to survive."],
    ["Locke's answer: memory", "John Locke argued that personal identity depends on consciousness and memory, not on the body.", "The English philosopher John Locke, writing in the late 1600s, offered an influential answer. A person, he suggested, is a thinking being who can consider itself as itself at different times and places. What makes you the same person as someone in the past is that you can remember, from the inside, that person's experiences. Locke illustrated this with a story in which the consciousness of a prince enters the body of a cobbler. The result, he said, would be the prince in a cobbler's body, not the cobbler. Locke's view captures something many people feel: that what matters is the continuing stream of mind, not the particular atoms. It also explains why memory loss can seem so frightening, because it threatens the self."],
    ["Problems with memory", "Critics pointed out that memory-based identity leads to contradictions and leaves gaps.", "Locke's theory soon met objections. The Scottish philosopher Thomas Reid offered a famous one, often called the brave officer. A boy is flogged for stealing; as a young officer he remembers the flogging; as an old general he remembers being the officer but has forgotten the flogging. On Locke's view the general is the officer and the officer is the boy, yet the general is not the boy. That seems to break the basic logic of 'same as'. Others noted that we forget most of our lives, including much of early childhood, yet we do not think we were someone else as babies. Later philosophers repaired the theory by appealing to overlapping chains of memory and psychological connection, rather than direct recall of every moment."],
    ["The chariot and the self", "Some Buddhist thinkers argued there is no fixed self at all, only a changing collection of parts.", "Long before Locke, Buddhist thinkers had approached the puzzle from another direction. A well-known text, the Questions of King Milinda, records a dialogue between a Greek-descended king and a monk named Nagasena. Nagasena asks the king whether his chariot is the axle, the wheels, the frame or the reins. None of them alone is the chariot, the king agrees, nor is the chariot something separate from them. 'Chariot' is a convenient name for parts working together. In the same way, Nagasena argues, 'Nagasena' is a name for a changing combination of body, feelings, perceptions and mental activity, with no unchanging core self underneath. This idea, called anatta or non-self, is central to many Buddhist traditions, which see letting go of attachment to a fixed self as a path away from suffering."],
    ["Teleporters and what matters", "Derek Parfit used thought experiments about teleportation to argue that identity may matter less than we think.", "The British philosopher Derek Parfit, in his 1984 book Reasons and Persons, sharpened these puzzles with science fiction. Imagine a teleporter that scans your body, destroys it and builds an exact copy on Mars, with all your memories and character. Is the person on Mars you, or a new person who merely thinks they are you? Now suppose the machine malfunctions and the original is not destroyed. Which one is you? Parfit concluded that there may sometimes be no fact of the matter, and that what really matters is psychological continuity and connection, not identity itself. He found this freeing, saying it made him care less about his own death. Many philosophers disagree with him, and the debate remains open, but his questions are hard to forget."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to Plutarch, what did the Athenians do with Theseus's ship?", ["Burned it as an offering", "Sank it in the harbour", "Kept it, replacing timbers as they decayed", "Sold it to Crete"], 2, "Plutarch wrote that the ship was preserved by replacing old planks with new ones."],
    [1, "What complication did Thomas Hobbes add to the puzzle?", ["The ship sinks", "The old planks are reassembled into a second ship", "Theseus returns to claim it", "The ship is painted a new colour"], 1, "Hobbes imagined the discarded planks rebuilt into a ship, leaving two rivals for the title."],
    [3, "What did John Locke think personal identity depends on?", ["Consciousness and memory", "The same body", "The same soul substance", "A person's name"], 0, "Locke argued that being the same person depends on consciousness, especially memory of past experiences."],
    [5, "What point does Nagasena make with the chariot?", ["Chariots are better than ships", "Kings should travel humbly", "The self is located in the heart", "There is no fixed self, only a changing combination of parts"], 3, "Like 'chariot', the self is a label for working parts, not an unchanging core."],
    [6, "What did Derek Parfit conclude from teleporter thought experiments?", ["Teleporters are impossible", "The soul always stays with the original body", "Psychological continuity may matter more than identity itself", "Memory has nothing to do with the self"], 2, "Parfit argued that what matters is psychological continuity and connection, not identity as such."]
  ]);

WB.L("descartes-doubt", "philosophy", "beginner", "Descartes: I think, therefore I am", "What if everything you believe is a dream, or a trick? René Descartes set out to doubt everything he possibly could, and what was left became one of the most famous sentences ever written.",
  [
    ["A man in a warm room", "René Descartes decided to tear down his beliefs and rebuild knowledge on a foundation that could not be doubted.", "In the winter of 1619, a young Frenchman named René Descartes was stuck in Germany, serving with an army. By his own account, he spent a day alone in a room heated by a stove, thinking. He had been educated at one of the best schools in Europe, yet he felt that much of what he had learned was uncertain. Scholars disagreed about nearly everything. Descartes, who was also a brilliant mathematician, admired the certainty of geometry, where proofs build step by step from secure starting points. He resolved to do something similar with all knowledge: to clear away every belief that could be doubted and see whether anything solid remained to build on. Years later he set out this project in his most famous works."],
    ["Doubting the senses", "Descartes began by noting that the senses sometimes deceive us, so they cannot be the foundation of certain knowledge.", "In his Meditations on First Philosophy, published in 1641, Descartes started with the senses. We trust them constantly, yet they sometimes mislead us. A tower looks round from far away and square up close. A straight stick looks bent in water. If a source of information has fooled you even once, he reasoned, it is not wise to trust it completely. This was not a claim that we should stop using our senses in daily life. Descartes was clear that his doubt was a tool for a special purpose, to be used once in a lifetime in a quiet hour of reflection. The aim was not to live as a sceptic but to find out what, if anything, could survive the strongest possible doubt."],
    ["The dream and the demon", "Descartes asked how we could know we are not dreaming, or being fooled by a powerful deceiver.", "Descartes then raised a stronger worry. He had often dreamed he was sitting by the fire in his dressing gown when he was actually asleep in bed. Is there any sure sign that tells waking life from a vivid dream? If not, even the scene in front of him might be unreal. Finally, he imagined the most extreme possibility: an evil demon, supremely powerful and cunning, devoting all its energy to deceiving him about everything, perhaps even about simple sums. Modern philosophers have updated the demon into a brain in a vat, kept alive and fed false experiences by scientists, an idea that inspired films such as The Matrix. The question is the same: can you prove this is not happening to you right now?"],
    ["The one certainty", "Descartes concluded that even if he is deceived about everything else, he must exist in order to be deceived.", "Here Descartes found his foothold. Suppose the demon is deceiving him about everything. Still, to be deceived, he must exist. To doubt, he must be thinking. However far the doubt is pushed, it cannot reach the fact that there is a doubter. In the Meditations he put it as 'I am, I exist', which must be true whenever he thinks it. In his earlier Discourse on the Method of 1637, written in French, he expressed it in the form that became famous, usually rendered in Latin as cogito, ergo sum: I think, therefore I am. Interestingly, the early Christian thinker Augustine had made a similar point more than a thousand years earlier, arguing that if he was mistaken, he must exist to be mistaken."],
    ["Rebuilding the world", "From the cogito, Descartes tried to prove God's existence and then restore trust in clear reasoning and the outside world.", "Knowing only that you exist is a lonely position. Descartes tried to climb out of it. He argued that he had an idea of a perfect being, and that such an idea could only have come from a being that really was perfect, namely God. A perfect God would not be a deceiver, he reasoned, so the things he perceived very clearly and distinctly could be trusted, and the physical world was real after all. Many readers have found this the weakest part of his project. Critics in his own time pointed to the so-called Cartesian circle: he seems to rely on clear and distinct ideas to prove God exists, then rely on God to guarantee clear and distinct ideas. Defenders have offered various replies, and scholars still debate them."],
    ["Mind and body", "Descartes argued that mind and body are two different kinds of thing, which raised the question of how they interact.", "Descartes's method led him to a view called dualism. He could doubt that he had a body, he reasoned, but not that he was thinking, so the mind must be a different kind of thing from the body: a thinking substance rather than an extended, physical one. This raised an obvious problem, which was put to him sharply by Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia in letters beginning in 1643. If the mind is not physical, how can it move the arm, or feel the burn of a flame? Descartes suggested the two met in the pineal gland in the brain, an answer few found satisfying. Most philosophers and scientists today reject his form of dualism, though questions about how mind relates to brain remain lively."],
    ["Why he still matters", "Descartes is often called the father of modern philosophy because he made the question of how we know things central.", "Descartes died in Stockholm in 1650, not long after moving there at the invitation of Queen Christina of Sweden. His influence was enormous. By starting from the individual mind and asking what it can know for certain, he pushed questions about knowledge, called epistemology, to the centre of philosophy for centuries. Later thinkers such as John Locke and David Hume answered him by stressing experience rather than pure reason. He also helped shape modern science and gave us coordinate geometry, the familiar x and y axes, which is why they are called Cartesian coordinates. Even people who reject his conclusions still use his method: when in doubt, slow down, question your assumptions and ask what you really have good reason to believe."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Why did Descartes think the senses could not be the foundation of certain knowledge?", ["They are too slow", "They sometimes deceive us", "They only work in daylight", "The Church forbade it"], 1, "Since the senses have misled us before, Descartes reasoned they cannot be trusted completely."],
    [2, "What is the modern update of Descartes's evil demon?", ["The brain in a vat", "The ship of Theseus", "The trolley problem", "The veil of ignorance"], 0, "The brain in a vat imagines scientists feeding a brain false experiences, much like the demon."],
    [3, "What can Descartes be certain of, even if a demon deceives him?", ["That mathematics is true", "That his body exists", "That God exists", "That he exists, because he is thinking"], 3, "To be deceived or to doubt, he must exist and be thinking."],
    [4, "What is the 'Cartesian circle' criticism?", ["Descartes drew circles to prove theorems", "He used God to guarantee clear ideas and clear ideas to prove God", "He argued in circles about geometry", "He travelled in a circle around Europe"], 1, "Critics argued his reasoning was circular, relying on what it was meant to prove."],
    [5, "Who challenged Descartes on how an immaterial mind could move the body?", ["Queen Christina", "Augustine", "Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia", "Thomas Hobbes"], 2, "Elisabeth pressed Descartes on this question in letters beginning in 1643."]
  ]);

WB.L("epicurus-pleasure", "philosophy", "beginner", "Epicurus: the surprising philosophy of pleasure", "Today 'epicurean' suggests fine wine and rich food. But Epicurus, the philosopher behind the word, lived simply in a garden, ate bread and water, and taught that the secret of happiness was needing less.",
  [
    ["The Garden", "Around 306 BC Epicurus founded a school in a garden outside Athens, open to women and enslaved people as well as citizens.", "Epicurus was born on the Greek island of Samos in 341 BC. After years of study and teaching elsewhere, he bought a house with a garden near Athens, and his followers became known as the Garden. It was an unusual community for its time. Ancient sources say it welcomed women and enslaved people, who were usually excluded from philosophical schools. Members lived simply and placed great value on friendship. The ancient biographer Diogenes Laertius reports that Epicurus lived mainly on bread and water, and once asked a friend to send a little pot of cheese so that he could have a feast whenever he liked. The man whose name now suggests luxury apparently thought a piece of cheese was a treat."],
    ["Pleasure as the goal", "Epicurus taught that pleasure is the goal of life, but he meant mainly the absence of pain and worry.", "Epicurus held that pleasure is the beginning and end of a happy life. That sounds like an invitation to indulge, and his enemies, ancient and modern, often presented it that way. But Epicurus defined pleasure in a particular way. The highest pleasure, for him, was a calm state of body and mind: freedom from physical pain, which he called aponia, and freedom from mental disturbance, called ataraxia. A banquet might bring a burst of enjoyment, followed by a hangover, debt or jealousy. A simple meal with friends, eaten without anxiety, could bring deeper and more lasting contentment. In his surviving Letter to Menoeceus, he explicitly says that by pleasure he does not mean the pleasures of the profligate."],
    ["Sorting your desires", "Epicurus divided desires into natural and necessary, natural but unnecessary, and empty ones that can never be satisfied.", "A practical tool at the heart of Epicureanism is a way of sorting desires. Some are natural and necessary, like the need for food, shelter and friendship. These are easy to satisfy and bring real peace. Some are natural but not necessary, like the desire for fancy food: fine to enjoy when they come along, but not worth chasing. A third kind he regarded as empty or vain, like the desire for fame, power or limitless wealth. These are dangerous because they have no natural limit. However much you get, there is always more to want, so they generate endless anxiety. The lesson sounds strikingly modern. Advertising and social media often work by turning unnecessary desires into what feel like needs."],
    ["Atoms and the gods", "Epicurus believed everything is made of atoms moving in empty space, and that the gods do not interfere in human life.", "Epicurus built his ethics on a view of nature borrowed and adapted from the earlier philosopher Democritus. Everything, he taught, is made of tiny indivisible particles called atoms moving through empty space. The universe was not designed and is not steered by divine purpose. He did not deny that gods existed, but he argued that perfectly happy beings would have no interest in punishing or rewarding humans. Thunderstorms, earthquakes and disease have natural causes. This mattered for happiness, because he believed fear of angry gods was a major source of misery. Later writers, such as the Roman poet Lucretius in his long poem On the Nature of Things, carried these ideas forward, and the poem's rediscovery in 1417 influenced Renaissance and early scientific thinking."],
    ["Nothing to fear in death", "Epicurus argued that death is nothing to us, because when death is present we no longer exist to experience it.", "Epicurus's most famous argument is about death. In the Letter to Menoeceus he says that death is nothing to us, since all good and bad lie in sensation, and death is the end of sensation. When we exist, death is not present; when death is present, we do not exist. So there is no moment at which death can harm the one who has died. The Roman Lucretius added the symmetry argument: we do not feel distress about the endless time before we were born, so why fear the endless time after? Many people find these arguments comforting; others say they miss the point, because what we dread is losing the good things life would have held. Philosophers still debate whether death can harm the one who dies."],
    ["The four-part cure", "Later Epicureans summed up the teaching in a 'fourfold remedy' for anxiety.", "Later followers condensed Epicurus's teaching into a short formula, known as the tetrapharmakos or fourfold remedy, found in the writings of the Epicurean philosopher Philodemus. In a common translation it runs: don't fear god, don't worry about death, what is good is easy to get, and what is terrible is easy to endure. The last line reflects Epicurus's view that intense pain tends to be brief, while long-lasting pain is usually bearable. Modern readers may find that too optimistic, especially about chronic illness. But the overall approach, treating philosophy as medicine for the mind, has parallels in modern therapy, which also encourages people to question the beliefs that feed their worries. A gravestone formula popular in the Roman world captured the mood: I was not, I was, I am not, I do not care."],
    ["Friends and quiet living", "Epicurus prized friendship and advised withdrawing from politics, a view that drew both admiration and criticism.", "Of all the things wisdom provides for a happy life, Epicurus is reported to have said, the greatest is friendship. The Garden was built around shared meals and conversation. He also recommended 'living unnoticed', keeping away from politics and public competition, which he saw as breeding fear and rivalry. Critics, including the Stoics and later the Roman statesman Cicero, accused the Epicureans of selfishness and of neglecting civic duty. Christian writers often attacked their denial of an afterlife. Yet the philosophy never quite disappeared. The American founder Thomas Jefferson called himself an Epicurean in a letter late in life. Today, in an age of burnout and endless wanting, its central question feels fresh: what do you actually need to be content?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was unusual about Epicurus's Garden for its time?", ["It was located in Rome", "It welcomed women and enslaved people", "It banned friendship", "It charged huge fees"], 1, "Ancient sources say the Garden admitted women and enslaved people, who were usually excluded."],
    [1, "What did Epicurus mean by the highest pleasure?", ["Luxurious feasting", "Winning public fame", "Freedom from pain and mental disturbance", "Constant excitement"], 2, "He valued aponia and ataraxia: freedom from bodily pain and mental disturbance."],
    [2, "Which desire would Epicurus class as empty or vain?", ["The desire for limitless wealth", "The desire for water", "The desire for shelter", "The desire for friendship"], 0, "Desires like fame and limitless wealth have no natural limit, so they breed anxiety."],
    [4, "What is Epicurus's argument about death?", ["Death leads to reward or punishment", "Death should be feared above all", "Death is only for the unwise", "When death is present we no longer exist to experience it"], 3, "Since death is the end of sensation, it cannot harm the one who has died."],
    [5, "What was the tetrapharmakos?", ["A Greek medicine made from herbs", "A fourfold remedy summarising Epicurean teaching", "A type of atom", "Epicurus's house"], 1, "It condensed the teaching into four lines about gods, death, good and suffering."]
  ]);

WB.L("ubuntu-philosophy", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Ubuntu: I am because we are", "A person is a person through other people. This idea from southern Africa helped guide a nation through its transition from apartheid, and it offers a challenge to the way many Western philosophies picture the self.",
  [
    ["A greeting that says a lot", "Ubuntu is an idea from southern Africa holding that we become fully human through our relationships with others.", "In many parts of southern Africa, a common way of greeting someone translates roughly as 'I see you', and the reply as 'I am here'. The exchange hints at a philosophy in which existence is affirmed through recognition by others. That philosophy is often summed up in the word ubuntu, found in Nguni languages such as isiZulu and isiXhosa, with related words across many Bantu languages. Ubuntu is usually translated as 'humanness' or 'humanity towards others'. It is captured in the isiZulu saying umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu, commonly translated as 'a person is a person through other persons'. Ubuntu is not one written theory by one founding philosopher. It is a living ethical outlook, carried in proverbs, customs and everyday expectations, that thinkers have more recently set out in systematic form."],
    ["The relational self", "Ubuntu sees personhood as something achieved through relationships, not simply possessed by an isolated individual.", "Many influential Western philosophies start with the individual. Descartes began with a single mind doubting everything; social contract theorists imagined separate individuals agreeing to form a society. Ubuntu starts somewhere else. It treats people as fundamentally bound up with one another. On many accounts, personhood is not just a biological fact but something you grow into by how you treat others. In everyday speech in some communities, a person who behaves cruelly or selfishly may be said to lack ubuntu, or even to be 'not a person', meaning not living up to what a human being should be. The Kenyan born philosopher John Mbiti, writing in 1969, put a related African communal outlook memorably: 'I am, because we are; and since we are, therefore I am.'"],
    ["What it asks of you", "Ubuntu emphasises values such as generosity, hospitality, compassion, respect and the pursuit of harmony.", "In practice, ubuntu is associated with a cluster of values: sharing with those in need, welcoming strangers, showing respect to elders, and repairing damaged relationships rather than simply punishing. A frequently told illustration is of a traveller passing through a village, who would expect to be offered food and water without having to ask. The South African philosopher Thaddeus Metz has tried to state the core of ubuntu precisely. He suggests that, on an ubuntu view, right actions are those that respect and promote harmonious relationships, where people identify with one another and act for each other's good. Wrong actions are those that are unfriendly or divisive. That makes ubuntu different from both rule-based and outcome-based ethics: the focus is on the quality of relationships."],
    ["Ubuntu and reconciliation", "Ubuntu played a prominent role in South Africa's transition from apartheid, especially through the Truth and Reconciliation Commission.", "Ubuntu became internationally known during South Africa's transition to democracy in the 1990s. The country's interim Constitution of 1993 ended with a passage saying there was 'a need for ubuntu but not for victimisation'. Archbishop Desmond Tutu, who chaired the Truth and Reconciliation Commission set up after apartheid, often explained his approach in terms of ubuntu. Rather than relying only on trials and punishment, the commission offered amnesty to some perpetrators who fully disclosed politically motivated crimes, and gave victims a public platform to tell their stories. Tutu argued that because humanity is shared, the dehumanising of others also diminishes the oppressor, and healing requires restoring relationships. The commission remains controversial: some victims felt justice was sacrificed, while others saw it as a remarkable alternative to cycles of revenge."],
    ["Ubuntu in law", "South African courts have drawn on ubuntu in landmark rulings, including the 1995 decision to abolish the death penalty.", "Ubuntu has also shaped South African law. In 1995, in the case known as S v Makwanyane, the new Constitutional Court ruled the death penalty unconstitutional. Several judges referred to ubuntu, arguing that it values life and human dignity and favours rehabilitation and reconciliation over retribution. Courts have since invoked ubuntu in other areas, from evictions to defamation, often to encourage dialogue and apology rather than purely financial penalties. Legal scholars debate how useful this is. Supporters say ubuntu grounds the constitution in local values rather than borrowed ones. Critics worry that the concept is too broad to give judges clear guidance and that it can be used to justify almost any conclusion. Both sides agree it has given the idea new public weight."],
    ["Criticisms and debates", "Critics argue ubuntu can be vague, romanticised or used to pressure people into conformity.", "Like any philosophy, ubuntu has thoughtful critics, including African philosophers. Some argue the term has become a slogan, used in corporate training and marketing until it means little more than 'teamwork'. Others warn against romanticising a single 'African' worldview across a vast continent with hundreds of cultures and long traditions of debate. A deeper philosophical worry concerns individual rights. If personhood depends on community, what protects people who dissent, or who are marginalised by their own communities, such as women in some traditional settings? Defenders reply that ubuntu, properly understood, condemns treating anyone as less than human, and that respecting individuals is part of maintaining genuine harmony. The Ghanaian philosopher Kwame Gyekye proposed a 'moderate communitarianism' that tries to give both community and individual rights their due."],
    ["A wider conversation", "Ubuntu is part of a rich tradition of African philosophy that is increasingly studied around the world.", "Ubuntu is one strand in a broad field of African philosophy. The Ghanaian philosopher Kwasi Wiredu urged what he called conceptual decolonisation: examining ideas inherited from colonial languages and asking whether African languages carve up concepts like truth or mind differently. The Kenyan philosopher Henry Odera Oruka recorded the reasoning of respected local sages, arguing that critical philosophy existed in oral cultures, not only in written ones. These thinkers challenge the old assumption that philosophy is a mainly European story. Ubuntu also invites comparison with other relational outlooks, such as the Confucian idea that we are defined by our roles and relationships. Whatever you conclude, it poses a question worth asking: how much of who you are was made by other people?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the saying umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu commonly mean?", ["A person is a person through other persons", "Every person stands alone", "The strong rule the weak", "Respect your ancestors"], 0, "This isiZulu saying is the classic summary of ubuntu."],
    [1, "Who wrote 'I am, because we are; and since we are, therefore I am'?", ["Desmond Tutu", "Nelson Mandela", "Kwasi Wiredu", "John Mbiti"], 3, "John Mbiti expressed this communal outlook in a 1969 book."],
    [2, "According to Thaddeus Metz's interpretation, what makes an action right on an ubuntu view?", ["It maximises total pleasure", "It respects and promotes harmonious relationships", "It follows a written code", "It benefits the person acting"], 1, "Metz suggests ubuntu judges actions by whether they promote harmonious relationships."],
    [4, "In S v Makwanyane in 1995, what did South Africa's Constitutional Court decide?", ["To create the Truth Commission", "To adopt ubuntu as the national motto", "To abolish the death penalty", "To end apartheid"], 2, "Several judges cited ubuntu when ruling the death penalty unconstitutional."],
    [5, "What concern do some critics raise about ubuntu?", ["It is too focused on individual rights", "It has no connection to Africa", "It forbids hospitality", "It can be vague or pressure people into conformity"], 3, "Critics worry it can become a slogan or be used to override dissenting individuals."]
  ]);

WB.L("islamic-golden-age-philosophy", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Philosophy's golden age in the Islamic world", "Between Ancient Greece and the European Renaissance, some of the boldest philosophy on Earth was written in Arabic. Meet the thinkers who preserved Aristotle, argued about cause and effect, and imagined a man floating in the air.",
  [
    ["Translating the Greeks", "From the 8th century, scholars in the Abbasid world translated huge amounts of Greek philosophy and science into Arabic.", "In the late 700s and 800s, under the Abbasid caliphs based in Baghdad, a remarkable translation movement got under way. Scholars, many of them Christians fluent in Greek and Syriac, translated the works of Aristotle, Plato's ideas as passed down by later writers, and Greek medicine, astronomy and mathematics into Arabic. Wealthy patrons paid well for this work. Baghdad's House of Wisdom is often associated with the movement, although historians debate how far it was a formal academy rather than a palace library. The result was that a great deal of Greek learning, much of it scarcely read in Western Europe at the time, became part of a lively intellectual culture stretching from Central Asia to Spain. Thinkers did not just preserve these texts; they argued with them."],
    ["The first philosophers", "Al-Kindi and al-Farabi set out to show how Greek philosophy related to Islamic belief and to politics.", "Al-Kindi, who worked in Baghdad in the 800s, is often called 'the philosopher of the Arabs'. He argued that truth should be welcomed whatever its source, even if it came from foreign peoples, and tried to show that philosophy and revelation pointed in the same direction. A generation or two later, al-Farabi, who probably came from Central Asia, became so respected as an interpreter of Aristotle that he was known as 'the Second Teacher', Aristotle being the first. Al-Farabi wrote on logic, music and especially politics. Inspired partly by Plato's Republic, he described a 'virtuous city' led by a wise ruler who would guide citizens towards true happiness. His work shaped how later thinkers connected philosophy, religion and the good society."],
    ["Avicenna", "Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, was one of history's most influential philosophers and physicians.", "Ibn Sina was born around 980 near Bukhara, in what is now Uzbekistan. He was famously precocious: in his autobiography he says he had mastered most of the sciences by eighteen, and that he read Aristotle's Metaphysics many times without understanding it until a commentary by al-Farabi made it click. His Canon of Medicine was used as a medical textbook in Europe for centuries. In philosophy he built a vast system of his own. One influential idea was a distinction between what a thing is, its essence, and the fact that it exists. You can understand what a unicorn is without knowing whether any exist. From this he argued for a necessary being, whose essence includes existence, which he identified with God."],
    ["The floating man", "Avicenna imagined a person created in mid-air with no sensations, to argue that we know our own existence directly.", "Avicenna's most famous thought experiment asks you to imagine a person created suddenly and fully formed, but suspended in the air, blindfolded, with limbs spread so that they do not touch each other, and with no sensations of any kind. Would this person know that they exist? Avicenna argued yes. Even with no awareness of a body or the outside world, they would be aware of their own self. He took this to show that the self is something distinct from the body. Readers often notice the similarity to Descartes's argument six centuries later. Whether Descartes knew of it is debated. Critics of both arguments question whether being able to imagine yourself without a body really shows that you could exist without one."],
    ["Al-Ghazali's challenge", "Al-Ghazali argued that the philosophers had gone too far, and questioned whether we ever see true cause and effect.", "Not everyone welcomed the philosophers' confidence. Abu Hamid al-Ghazali, a celebrated scholar of the late 1000s, wrote The Incoherence of the Philosophers, attacking Avicenna and others on several points, including their claim that the world had always existed. His most striking argument concerned causation. When cotton touches fire, it burns. But what do we actually observe? Only that burning follows contact. We never see a necessary connection forcing it. Al-Ghazali concluded that God directly creates each event, usually in regular patterns, so that miracles are possible. Centuries later the Scottish philosopher David Hume made a similar observation about cause and effect, though for very different reasons. Al-Ghazali also wrote movingly about a personal crisis of doubt that led him to leave a prestigious teaching post and seek certainty through mysticism."],
    ["Averroes replies", "Ibn Rushd, known as Averroes, defended philosophy and wrote commentaries on Aristotle that shaped medieval Europe.", "In Córdoba, in Muslim Spain, the judge and physician Ibn Rushd, known in Latin as Averroes, answered al-Ghazali in the 1100s with a book whose title is usually translated The Incoherence of the Incoherence. He argued that al-Ghazali had misrepresented the philosophers, and that religious law itself encouraged people capable of it to study nature through reason. If scripture seemed to conflict with sound demonstration, he said, it should be read allegorically. His greatest legacy was his detailed commentaries on Aristotle. Translated into Latin, they became so influential in European universities that Thomas Aquinas referred to him simply as 'the Commentator'. Some of his ideas, as interpreted by European followers, caused fierce controversy in Paris in the 1200s. Dante placed him among the great thinkers of the past in the Divine Comedy."],
    ["A shared inheritance", "The Arabic philosophical tradition linked Greek, Jewish, Christian and Muslim thinkers and continued long after the 12th century.", "This was not only a Muslim story. The Jewish philosopher Moses Maimonides, born in Córdoba and later working in Egypt, wrote his Guide for the Perplexed in Arabic, engaging closely with Aristotle and Islamic philosophy. Christian scholars in Spain translated Arabic works into Latin, feeding the rise of medieval European universities. Ibn Tufayl wrote a philosophical novel about a boy raised alone on an island who reasons his way to deep truths; it is often thought to have been known in Europe by the time of Robinson Crusoe. It is also a myth that Islamic philosophy ended in the 1100s. It continued to flourish, especially in Persia, with thinkers such as Mulla Sadra in the 1600s. These debates on reason, faith and the self belong to everyone's intellectual history."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In which city were the Abbasid caliphs based during the great translation movement?", ["Cairo", "Córdoba", "Baghdad", "Damascus"], 2, "The Abbasid caliphs ruled from Baghdad, where much translation from Greek into Arabic took place."],
    [1, "Why was al-Farabi called 'the Second Teacher'?", ["He was a highly respected interpreter of Aristotle, the first teacher", "He was al-Kindi's student", "He taught for only two years", "He wrote two books"], 0, "Aristotle was regarded as the First Teacher, and al-Farabi as his great successor."],
    [3, "What did Avicenna's floating man thought experiment aim to show?", ["That gravity does not exist", "That the senses are perfectly reliable", "That we know our own existence without bodily sensation", "That God created the world in time"], 2, "Avicenna argued the floating person would still be aware of their own self."],
    [4, "What example did al-Ghazali use when discussing cause and effect?", ["An apple falling", "Cotton touching fire", "A ship at sea", "A ball striking another"], 1, "He argued we only see burning follow contact, not a necessary connection between them."],
    [5, "What did Thomas Aquinas call Averroes?", ["The Floating Man", "The Second Teacher", "The Incoherent", "The Commentator"], 3, "His commentaries on Aristotle were so influential that Aquinas called him simply 'the Commentator'."]
  ]);

WB.L("problem-of-induction", "philosophy", "advanced", "Hume's problem: why trust that the sun will rise?", "You are sure the sun will rise tomorrow. But can you prove it? David Hume's simple question about learning from experience has troubled philosophers and scientists for nearly three centuries, and a black swan from Western Australia became its mascot.",
  [
    ["The question nobody asked", "David Hume asked what justifies our expectation that the future will resemble the past.", "In 1739 a young Scottish philosopher named David Hume published A Treatise of Human Nature, which he later said 'fell dead-born from the press'. In it, and in a shorter book in 1748, An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, he raised a question that seems almost silly at first. Why do we believe the sun will rise tomorrow? Because it always has, you might say. But that is exactly the problem. We have only observed the past. Our confidence about tomorrow rests on an assumption that nature will continue to behave as it has. Hume asked what justifies that assumption. His answer, that reason alone cannot, is now known as the problem of induction, and it remains one of philosophy's most famous unsolved puzzles."],
    ["Deduction and induction", "Deduction guarantees its conclusions, while induction moves from observed cases to general claims that could turn out false.", "To see the puzzle, it helps to separate two kinds of reasoning. In deduction, if the premises are true, the conclusion must be true. All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; so Socrates is mortal. There is no way around it. Induction is different. It moves from observed cases to general rules or predictions: every piece of copper I have heated has expanded, so copper expands when heated. However many cases you observe, the conclusion could still be false. Science, medicine, cooking and crossing the road all depend on induction. We could not survive without it. Hume did not suggest we stop using it. He asked a narrower question: can we give a good argument that inductive reasoning is reliable, one that does not simply assume what it is trying to prove?"],
    ["The circle", "Any argument that induction works seems to rely on induction itself, which is circular.", "Hume's argument runs roughly like this. Any justification for induction must be either a deductive proof or an argument from experience. It cannot be deductive, because there is no contradiction in imagining the future being different: you can coherently picture the sun failing to rise, or bread suddenly ceasing to nourish. So it must come from experience. But an argument from experience would go: induction has worked well in the past, so it will work in the future. That is itself an inductive argument, so it assumes the very thing in question. Hume concluded that our expectations come not from reason but from custom or habit. After seeing one event follow another many times, the mind simply comes to expect it. This, he thought, is how human nature works."],
    ["Chickens and black swans", "Bertrand Russell's chicken and the discovery of black swans in Australia show how expectations built on past experience can fail.", "The British philosopher Bertrand Russell gave a grim illustration in 1912. A chicken is fed every day by the farmer, and comes to expect food whenever he appears. One day, the farmer wrings its neck instead. Its past experience was real, but it misled the chicken completely. A more cheerful example comes from Australia. For centuries Europeans used 'black swan' as a phrase for something impossible, because every swan they had seen was white. In 1697 the Dutch explorer Willem de Vlamingh's expedition recorded black swans on a river in Western Australia, which they named the Swan River. A rule supported by countless observations was overturned by one. The black swan is now a common emblem of the limits of induction."],
    ["Popper's way out", "Karl Popper argued that science does not rely on induction, but advances by trying to falsify bold theories.", "The Austrian born philosopher Karl Popper, in work first published in 1934, offered a radical response. He agreed with Hume that induction cannot be justified. But science, he argued, does not actually need it. Scientists do not prove theories by piling up confirming cases. They propose bold conjectures and then try hard to refute them. A single genuine black swan can falsify 'all swans are white', and that falsification is a matter of deduction. Theories that survive tough tests are not proven true; they are simply the best we have so far. Popper also used falsifiability to separate science from what he called pseudo-science. Critics reply that in practice we still rely on theories that have survived testing to predict the future, which seems to bring induction back in."],
    ["The new riddle", "Nelson Goodman showed that even deciding which patterns to project into the future is a deep puzzle.", "In 1955 the American philosopher Nelson Goodman deepened the problem. Define a made-up word, 'grue', to mean green if first observed before some future date, and blue otherwise. Every emerald ever observed has been green. But every emerald ever observed has also been grue, since all were observed before that date. So the same evidence seems to support 'emeralds are green' and 'emeralds are grue', which make opposite predictions about emeralds first seen after the date. Obviously we should project green, not grue. But why? Saying grue is an artificial word does not settle it, because from a different starting vocabulary green could look artificial too. Goodman's new riddle shows that the hard part is not only trusting patterns but knowing which patterns count."],
    ["Living with uncertainty", "Philosophers still debate responses, from probability theory to pragmatism, but Hume's lesson about humility endures.", "Many responses to Hume have been attempted. Some philosophers argue that induction can be justified in terms of probability, using tools such as Bayesian reasoning to show how evidence should raise or lower our confidence, though critics say these methods still need assumptions about the world. Others take a pragmatic line: if any method can predict the future, induction will, so it is our best bet. Some argue that the demand for a non-circular justification is unreasonable, since the most basic forms of reasoning cannot all be proved without using themselves. Immanuel Kant said that reading Hume woke him from his 'dogmatic slumber'. Whatever view you take, Hume's lesson is practical: hold general beliefs with confidence proportional to the evidence, and stay open to the black swan."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What is the key difference between deduction and induction?", ["Deduction is used only in maths", "Induction is always more reliable", "True premises guarantee a deductive conclusion, but not an inductive one", "They are the same thing"], 2, "Deduction guarantees its conclusion; induction moves to conclusions that could still be false."],
    [2, "According to Hume, where do our expectations about the future really come from?", ["Custom or habit", "Pure logical proof", "Divine revelation", "Mathematical calculation"], 0, "Hume argued that repeated experience creates a habit of expectation, not a rational proof."],
    [3, "Where did a Dutch expedition record black swans in 1697?", ["New Zealand", "Tasmania", "Java", "Western Australia"], 3, "Willem de Vlamingh's expedition recorded black swans on what became the Swan River."],
    [4, "How did Karl Popper respond to the problem of induction?", ["He argued science advances by trying to falsify bold theories", "He proved induction was deductive", "He abandoned science", "He said habit is a form of proof"], 0, "Popper held that science works by conjecture and attempted refutation, not by proving theories inductively."],
    [5, "What does Goodman's word 'grue' show?", ["Emeralds change colour", "Choosing which patterns to project is itself a puzzle", "Colour words are meaningless", "Deduction is unreliable"], 1, "The same evidence supports 'green' and 'grue', so we need a reason to project one pattern over another."]
  ]);

WB.L("veil-of-ignorance", "philosophy", "advanced", "The veil of ignorance: designing a fair society", "Imagine designing the rules of society without knowing whether you would be born rich or poor, healthy or sick. John Rawls's thought experiment reshaped debates about justice, and drew powerful critics who saw things very differently.",
  [
    ["A game of chance", "John Rawls asked what rules people would choose for society if they did not know their own place in it.", "Imagine you are about to be born into a society, but you do not yet know who you will be. You might be born into wealth or poverty, with great talents or a serious disability, into a majority group or a minority. Before you find out, you get to help choose the basic rules of that society: how wealth is shared, which freedoms are protected, how opportunities are distributed. What rules would you pick? This is the heart of the most famous thought experiment in modern political philosophy, set out by the American philosopher John Rawls in his 1971 book A Theory of Justice. Rawls argued that the rules people would agree to in that situation of uncertainty are the rules of a just society."],
    ["Behind the veil", "Rawls called this imagined situation the original position, where a veil of ignorance hides each person's particular circumstances.", "Rawls drew on a long tradition of social contract thinking. Earlier philosophers such as Thomas Hobbes, John Locke and Jean-Jacques Rousseau imagined people agreeing to form a society. Hobbes famously described life without government as 'solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short'. Rawls's twist was to make the agreement fair by design. In what he called the original position, people choose principles from behind a 'veil of ignorance'. They know general facts about economics and human psychology, but not their own sex, class, abilities, religion or idea of a good life. The point is to strip away bias. If you do not know whether you will be rich, you cannot rig the rules to favour the rich. Whatever principles emerge should then be acceptable from every position."],
    ["Two principles", "Rawls argued people would choose equal basic liberties for all, and allow inequalities only under strict conditions.", "Rawls argued that people behind the veil would choose two principles. First, everyone should have an equal right to the most extensive basic liberties compatible with the same liberties for others, such as freedom of speech, conscience and political participation. Second, social and economic inequalities are acceptable only if two conditions are met. Positions must be open to all under fair equality of opportunity, meaning people with similar talent and effort should have similar chances regardless of the family they are born into. And inequalities must work to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged members of society. This last condition is the famous difference principle. Rawls ranked the first principle above the second, so that basic liberties could not be traded away for economic gain."],
    ["The difference principle", "The difference principle permits inequality only when it makes the worst-off better off than they would otherwise be.", "The difference principle is often misunderstood as demanding equal incomes. It does not. Rawls accepted that paying some people more, for example to encourage training or hard work, could make the whole economy more productive. If those gains lift up the worst-off, the inequality is justified. What it rules out is inequality that benefits those at the top without benefiting those at the bottom. Rawls thought people behind the veil would reason cautiously, focusing on how bad the worst outcome could be, because the stakes are a whole life and they have no basis for estimating the odds. Economists call this a 'maximin' strategy: maximise the minimum. Critics, such as the economist John Harsanyi, argued that rational people would instead choose to maximise average wellbeing."],
    ["Nozick's challenge", "Robert Nozick argued that justice depends on how holdings came about, not on matching a pattern of distribution.", "Rawls's most famous critic was his Harvard colleague Robert Nozick, whose 1974 book Anarchy, State, and Utopia defended a libertarian view. Nozick argued that a distribution of wealth is just if people acquired their holdings fairly and transferred them voluntarily, whatever pattern results. He illustrated this with a basketball star of the day, Wilt Chamberlain. Suppose society begins with a distribution you consider perfectly fair, and then a million fans each happily pay a small sum to watch Chamberlain play. He becomes rich, and the pattern is upset. Yet every step was voluntary. Nozick asked how the result could be unjust. He concluded that maintaining any fixed pattern would require continual interference in people's free choices, and that redistributive taxation risks treating people's labour as a resource others may claim."],
    ["Other critics", "Communitarians, feminists and capability theorists challenged Rawls from different directions.", "Criticism also came from other angles. Communitarian philosophers, such as Michael Sandel in a 1982 book, argued that the people behind the veil are 'unencumbered selves', stripped of the loyalties, traditions and communities that actually make us who we are, and that a theory built on them misunderstands human life. The feminist philosopher Susan Moller Okin argued that Rawls had not taken justice within the family seriously enough, even though families shape children's opportunities so profoundly. The economist and philosopher Amartya Sen, with the philosopher Martha Nussbaum, developed the capabilities approach, arguing that justice should focus less on resources and more on what people are actually able to do and be, since people with different needs convert the same income into very different lives."],
    ["Why it still matters", "Whatever its flaws, the veil of ignorance remains a practical tool for testing whether rules are fair.", "Rawls refined his views over the following decades, notably in Political Liberalism in 1993, where he asked how people with deeply different religious and moral beliefs can share a stable, fair society. His work and the responses to it frame much of today's argument about tax, welfare, health care and opportunity, across the political spectrum. Even people who reject his conclusions often find the veil of ignorance a useful thinking tool. When debating a policy, from school funding to disability support, you can ask: would I accept this rule if I did not know which side of it I would end up on? That question does not settle every disagreement, and reasonable people still reach different answers. But it pushes us to see a society from more than our own seat."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In which book did John Rawls set out the veil of ignorance?", ["Leviathan", "A Theory of Justice", "Anarchy, State, and Utopia", "Reasons and Persons"], 1, "Rawls presented the original position and veil of ignorance in A Theory of Justice in 1971."],
    [1, "What does the veil of ignorance hide from people choosing principles?", ["General facts about economics", "The laws of logic", "Their own particular circumstances, such as class and abilities", "The existence of other people"], 2, "People know general facts but not their own place in society, which removes bias."],
    [3, "What does the difference principle allow?", ["Inequalities that benefit the least advantaged", "No inequality of any kind", "Any inequality arising from markets", "Inequalities that benefit the most talented only"], 0, "Inequality is acceptable only if it works to the greatest benefit of the worst-off."],
    [4, "What point did Robert Nozick make with the Wilt Chamberlain example?", ["Sport should be free", "Taxes should fund stadiums", "Talented people deserve nothing", "Voluntary transfers can upset any pattern without seeming unjust"], 3, "Nozick argued that free exchanges from a fair start produce a just result, whatever the pattern."],
    [5, "What does the capabilities approach of Sen and Nussbaum focus on?", ["Equal incomes for all", "What people are actually able to do and be", "The traditions of a community", "Maximising average wellbeing"], 1, "It shifts attention from resources to people's real capabilities to live lives they value."]
  ]);
